## Installation & Setup

### Prerequisites
1. **Haxe Compiler:** [Haxe 4.3.0+](https://haxe.org/download/)
2. **Heaps Engine Library:**
   ```bash
   haxelib install heaps
   ```

### 1. Compilation
Build the production JavaScript bundle from the root of the project:
```bash
haxe compile.hxml
```
The output file `web/game.js` will be created or updated automatically.

### 2. Running Automated Tests

#### Headless Unit Test Suite (`utest`)
```bash
haxe test.hxml
```
Executes 197 assertions across 12 test fixtures (Audio, Camera, Version, Entities, Input, Physics, SplineMath, Save, Graphics, Touch, Track) in under 0.10s via Node.js. Fully compatible with the VSCode `Haxe Test Explorer` plugin (`test-adapter`).

#### Browser Visual & UI Automation Suite
```bash
node tests/run-browser-tests.js
```
Launches Microsoft Edge in headless mode with an isolated profile and runs automated scenarios via Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) testing startup resolution, decoupled UI vs 3D render scaling, graphics dependency locking, global sensitivity slider persistence, touch layout customization, and simulated in-game track driving with nitrous depletion/recharge.

You can also open `web/test-suite.html` directly in any web browser to view the interactive test dashboard and embedded live game execution.

### 3. Running Locally
Because WebGL, Web Audio, Service Workers, and Canvas WebP decoding require a valid HTTP origin, serve the `web/` directory using any local development server:

#### Using Python 3:
```bash
cd web
python -m http.server 8000
```

#### Using Node.js (`npx`):
```bash
npx serve web
```

Navigate to `http://localhost:8000` in any modern Chromium, Firefox, or WebKit browser.

